hylogenetic analyses of primate size evolution: the consequences of sexual selection</article-title><source>Biol J Linn Soc</source><year>1998</year><volume>64</volume><fpage>413</fpage><lpage>447</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1006/bijl.1998.0237</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barton</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Harvey</surname><given-names>PH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mosaic evolution of brain structure in mammals</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2000</year><volume>405</volume><fpage>1055</fpage><lpage>1058</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10890446</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/35016580</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Whiting</surname><given-names>BA</given-names></name><name><surname>Barton</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The evolution of the cortico-cerebellar complex in primates: anatomical connections predict patterns of correlated evolution</article-title><source>J Hum Evol</source><year>2003</year><volume>44</volume><fpage>3</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12604300</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0047-2484(02)00162-8</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pawlowski</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Lowen</surname><given-names>CB</given-names></name><name><surname>Dunbar</surname><given-names>RIM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Neocortex size, social skills and mating success in primates</article-title><source>Behaviour</source><year>1998</year><volume>135</volume><fpage>357</fpage><lpage>368</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Emlen</surname><given-names>ST</given-names></name><name><surname>Oring</surname><given-names>LW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ecology, sexual selection, and the evolution of mating systems</article-title><source>Science</source><year>1977</year><volume>197</volume><fpage>215</fpage><lpage>223</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">327542</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1126/science.327542</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>van Schaik</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Standen V, Foley RA</surname></name></person-group><article-title>The ecology of social relationships amongst female primates</article-title><source>Comparative Socioecology: The Behavioural Ecology of Humans and Other Mammals</source><year>1989</year><publisher-name>Oxford: Blackwell</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Altmann</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Primate males go where the females are</article-title><source>Anim Behav</source><year>1990</year><volume>39</volume><fpage>193</fpage><lpage>195</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0003-3472(05)80740-7</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smuts</surname><given-names>BB</given-names></name><name><surname>Cheney</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name><name><surname>Seyfarth</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name><name><surname>Wrangham</surname><given-names>RW</given-names></name><name><surname>Struhsaker</surname><given-names>TT</given-names></name></person-group><source>Primate societies</source><year>1987</year><publisher-name>Chicago: University of Chicago Press</publisher-n